{"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/AstronomyKit\/documentation\/AstronomyKit\/FixedStar"},"metadata":{"fragments":[{"text":"struct","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"FixedStar","kind":"identifier"}],"title":"FixedStar","symbolKind":"struct","role":"symbol","modules":[{"name":"AstronomyKit"}],"externalID":"s:12AstronomyKit9FixedStarV","navigatorTitle":[{"kind":"identifier","text":"FixedStar"}],"roleHeading":"Structure"},"primaryContentSections":[{"declarations":[{"tokens":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"struct"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"FixedStar"}],"languages":["swift"],"platforms":["macOS"]}],"kind":"declarations"},{"kind":"mentions","mentions":["doc:\/\/AstronomyKit\/documentation\/AstronomyKit\/CelestialPositions"]},{"kind":"content","content":[{"anchor":"overview","type":"heading","text":"Overview","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Fixed stars are celestial objects whose positions are essentially constant","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"on the celestial sphere. This struct stores catalog coordinates and provides","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"position calculations at any time.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Unlike solar system bodies (which require orbital calculations), fixed stars","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"are defined by their J2000 mean equator coordinates and distance. The library","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"handles precession, nutation, and aberration automatically. Ecliptic results","type":"text"},{"text":" ","type":"text"},{"text":"are in the true ecliptic and equinox of date, like the planet positions.","type":"text"}]},{"anchor":"Example","type":"heading","text":"Example","level":2},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":["let algol = FixedStar(","    name: \"Algol\",","    rightAscension: 3.136148,  \/\/ J2000 RA in hours","    declination: 40.9556,      \/\/ J2000 Dec in degrees","    distance: 92.95    \/\/ Light-years",")","","let longitude = try algol.eclipticLongitude(at: .now)","print(\"Algol is at \\(longitude)°\")"]},{"anchor":"Coordinate-Sources","type":"heading","text":"Coordinate Sources","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"J2000 coordinates can be found from:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"unorderedList","items":[{"content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"identifier":"https:\/\/simbad.cds.unistra.fr\/simbad\/","isActive":true,"type":"reference"}]}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Hipparcos Catalog","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]},{"content":[{"inlineContent":[{"text":"Yale Bright Star Catalog","type":"text"}],"type":"paragraph"}]}]},{"anchor":"Concurrency","type":"heading","text":"Concurrency","level":2},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"The C library exposes eight user-defined star slots ("},{"code":"BODY_STAR1","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"…"},{"code":"BODY_STAR8","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":"),"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"each an independent record. Calculations map a star to a slot by a stable hash of"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"its coordinates, so up to eight distinct stars can be computed fully in parallel."},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"A slot is only redefined when the star currently loaded in it differs, so repeated"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"calls for the same star skip the redefinition entirely. Stars whose hashes collide"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"on the same slot serialize on that slot’s mutex (correct, just slower)."}]}]}],"sections":[],"relationshipsSections":[{"kind":"relationships","title":"Conforms To","type":"conformsTo","identifiers":["doc:\/\/AstronomyKit\/s8CopyableP","doc:\/\/AstronomyKit\/s23CustomStringConvertibleP","doc:\/\/AstronomyKit\/SQ","doc:\/